# Copy to supabase/functions/.env for local development:
#   cp supabase/functions/.env.example supabase/functions/.env
#   supabase functions serve --env-file supabase/functions/.env --no-verify-jwt
#
# In production these are Supabase secrets, never files:
#   supabase secrets set APP_PIN_ARGON2_HASH='$argon2id$v=19$...'
#   supabase secrets set APP_PIN_BCRYPT_HASH='$2b$10$...'
#
# Generate BOTH hashes for a new PIN with:  npx tsx scripts/hash-pin.ts 1922
# (it prints the two lines below, ready to paste)
#
# The hashes below are for the trip PIN 1922 (Kyle's choice) and are committed so a fresh
# clone can run the stack with the real PIN. The repo is private and the PIN is a weak,
# deliberately-recoverable gate (everything behind it is an input; leaderboards re-derive),
# so committing the hash is an accepted tradeoff — see docs/spec/decisions.md §"PIN length".
# To keep the PIN out of git instead, set both only as Supabase secrets and leave a throwaway
# hash here.
# NOTE: the hash MUST be single-quoted. An argon2 encoded hash is full of `$`, and the
# CLI's --env-file parser performs shell-style variable expansion on unquoted values —
# a salt beginning `$GB...` had `$GB` expanded to nothing, silently corrupting the hash
# and making every unlock fail with a 500 "Invalid hash". Single quotes disable it.
# The same applies to `supabase secrets set` — quote the hash there too.
APP_PIN_ARGON2_HASH='$argon2id$v=19$m=19456,t=3,p=1$euJ5M4LLSApkZgDp9wF7oA$pdwyPpxLyvu1aYgJxrL++/LLQ8ZqPlyqiSHcnB+YoAc'
# The OFFLINE bcrypt hash for the same PIN 1922 (cost 10). Same quoting rule as above — a
# bcrypt hash is full of `$`, so single-quote it or --env-file expansion mangles it.
APP_PIN_BCRYPT_HASH='$2b$10$aYgxu5pjZFggTV/17JLYO.1XigzsDE1GUI9eEmIrNZGOl73DVKCuS'
APP_SESSION_EXPIRES_AT=2027-02-08T23:59:59-05:00
